# Layout Animations

Svelte Motion supports two kinds of layout animation:

- **`layout`** — animate a single element when its own size or position changes (FLIP).
- **`layoutId`** — animate between two different elements that share the same identifier. When one unmounts and another with the same `layoutId` mounts, the new element FLIP-animates from the old one's position.

## Single-element layout animation

Add `layout` to any motion element to have it automatically animate when its position or size changes in the DOM.

```svelte
<motion.div layout>
    <!-- This element will FLIP-animate whenever its bounding rect changes -->
</motion.div>
```

Use `layout="position"` to only animate translation (no scale):

```svelte
<motion.div layout="position">
    <!-- Translates smoothly, does not scale -->
</motion.div>
```

## Shared layout with `layoutId`

The `layoutId` prop enables shared layout animations between completely different elements. When element A with `layoutId="foo"` unmounts and element B with the same `layoutId="foo"` mounts, B automatically animates from A's last known position.

### Tab underline example

The most common use case is a tab indicator that slides between tabs:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { AnimatePresence, motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let selectedTab = $state(0)
    const tabs = ['Home', 'About', 'Contact']
</script>

{#each tabs as tab, i (tab)}
    <button onclick={() => (selectedTab = i)}>
        {tab}
        <AnimatePresence>
            {#if selectedTab === i}
                <motion.div
                    key="underline"
                    layoutId="underline"
                    style="position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 2px; background: royalblue;"
                    transition={{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 500, damping: 30 }}
                />
            {/if}
        </AnimatePresence>
    </button>
{/each}
```

### How it works

1. One element leads each `layoutId` at a time. A `layoutId` can change after mount: the element leaves its old id and joins the new one, as if it had unmounted and mounted again. Moving an id between mounted elements (`layoutId={selected ? 'highlight' : undefined}`) animates the new holder from the old one. An element that takes an id from one that is still on screen takes over instantly, and the other is hidden.
2. When the old element unmounts, its bounding rect is captured. Inside `AnimatePresence` it is held instead, and crossfades into the new one.
3. When the new element mounts, it reads the stored rect and performs a FLIP animation from the old position to its own natural position.
4. The animation uses the element's `transition` prop, or falls back to the previous element's transition.

### Crossfading with `AnimatePresence`

Inside `AnimatePresence`, the element that leaves a `layoutId` is held until the shared animation ends: it follows the new element and fades out while the new one fades in, as in Motion, even without an `exit` prop. See [AnimatePresence: Shared layout](/docs/animate-presence#shared-layout).

### Tabs without conditional rendering

Every tab can render its own highlight. Give `layoutId` only to the selected one, and the highlight slides to the new tab when the selection changes (Motion 14.7.1, #1411):

```svelte
<script>
    import { motion } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    const tabs = ['Home', 'Search', 'Profile']
    let selected = $state('Home')
</script>

{#each tabs as tab (tab)}
    <button onclick={() => (selected = tab)} style="position: relative;">
        {tab}
        <motion.div
            layoutId={selected === tab ? 'highlight' : undefined}
            style="position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 2px; background: {selected ===
            tab
                ? 'royalblue'
                : 'transparent'};"
        />
    </button>
{/each}
```

Layout animations can travel along a curve instead of a straight FLIP interpolation. See [`arc()`](/docs/arc) for `transition.layout.path` and shared `layoutId` examples.

